/scratch/gdemil24/CVPR_sentinel_Fusion/ISPR_Paper_latex/main.aux:224:\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{\textbf  {Evaluation Resolution and Protocol.} We explicitly clarify the resolution at which training and evaluation are conducted. During training, Sentinel-1 and Sentinel-2 inputs at native 10m resolution are aggregated to 60m through mean pooling over $6 \times 6$ pixel blocks to match the GFSC reference data resolution. The model predicts fractional snow cover at 60m resolution, and \textbf  {all quantitative metrics reported in this paper are computed at 60m resolution} against GFSC labels. This approach reflects the fundamental constraint that high-resolution ($<$60m) fractional snow cover ground truth is not widely available. The multi-resolution framework described in Section~\ref {sec:multi_resolution} enables optional inference-time disaggregation from 60m predictions to 10m outputs through guided upsampling that leverages high-resolution input features. However, we emphasize that the 10m outputs represent \textit  {spatially enhanced predictions} guided by fine-scale input structure, not independently validated high-resolution products. Qualitative assessment confirms that 10m predictions preserve boundary details consistent with Sentinel-2 optical imagery (see Figure~\ref {fig:qualitative}), but quantitative validation of sub-60m accuracy would require independent high-resolution ground truth from UAV surveys, airborne sensors, or alternative products such as Landsat-derived 30m snow cover, this remains an important direction for future work.}}{20}{section*.26}\protected@file@percent }
