\begin{figure}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=1\linewidth]{images//results/Prediction_main_result_s02.png}
    \caption{Representative qualitative results showing Sentinel-1 SAR dual-polarization backscatter (VV and VH) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery as model inputs (top row). Model outputs (bottom row) show fractional snow cover predictions, binary classification results, and spatially coherent uncertainty maps. The model successfully captures fine-scale spatial patterns with sharp boundaries at snow-land transitions. Uncertainty estimates appropriately increase at transition zones while maintaining low values in homogeneous regions, demonstrating excellent model calibration with uncertainty concentrated at snow-land boundaries and complex terrain.}
    \label{fig:qualitative}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:qualitative} presents representative test samples demonstrating our model's superior spatial detail preservation and uncertainty quantification. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:qualitative_comparison}, our hybrid architecture produces smoother, more realistic snow cover maps with accurate boundary delineation. Early Fusion exhibits noisy predictions with false positives in non-snow regions due to its inability to capture complex inter-modal relationships. CNN-Only achieves better smoothness but misses fine-scale snow patterns in complex terrain due to lack of global context. Transformer-Only produces spatially inconsistent results, lacking the local feature extraction necessary for precise boundary localization. XFuse preserves fine-scale snow boundaries, avoids false positives in forested areas, and captures gradual snow-cover gradients more faithfully than all baselines. The corresponding per-model error maps (Figure~\ref{fig:error_maps}) reveal that XFuse achieves substantially lower absolute errors at snow-land transition zones and forest edges --- precisely the regions where spatial context from the Transformer and bidirectional cross-attention provide the greatest benefit. The spatially coherent uncertainty maps provide operationally valuable confidence information, with elevated uncertainty at ambiguous boundaries and low uncertainty in homogeneous regions.

\begin{figure*}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/results/qualitative_comparison_all_models.png}
    \caption{\added{Qualitative comparison of all methods on three representative test scenes: (a) high snow coverage ($>$80\%), (b) transitional snow conditions ($\sim$40\%), (c) sparse snow ($<$20\%). Columns show Sentinel-1 VV backscatter, Sentinel-2 RGB composite, GFSC ground truth, and predictions from Early Fusion, CNN-Only, Transformer-Only, and XFuse (ours). XFuse preserves fine-scale snow boundaries (column 7 vs.\ columns 4--6), avoids false positives in forested areas, and captures gradual snow-cover gradients more faithfully than all baselines. Early Fusion produces spatially noisy predictions; CNN-Only over-smooths transitions; Transformer-Only misses local boundary detail.}}
    \label{fig:qualitative_comparison}
\end{figure*}

\begin{figure*}[!h]
    \centering
    \includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{images/results/error_maps_comparison.png}
    \caption{\added{Per-model absolute error maps for the three representative test scenes (same scenes as Figure~\ref{fig:qualitative_comparison}). Color scale: blue = low error ($<$5\%), red = high error ($>$30\%). XFuse (rightmost column) achieves substantially lower errors at snow-land transition zones and forest edges --- precisely the regions where spatial context from the Transformer and bidirectional cross-attention provide the greatest benefit. CNN-Only and Transformer-Only exhibit complementary failure modes (over-smoothed boundaries vs.\ spatially incoherent errors), both of which are mitigated in the hybrid XFuse architecture.}}
    \label{fig:error_maps}
\end{figure*}

\subsection{Robustness to Cloud Contamination}
\label{sec:cloud_robustness}

\added{Cloud contamination is the primary motivation for SAR-optical fusion: optical sensors cannot penetrate cloud cover, while Sentinel-1 SAR provides all-weather imaging capability. We validate XFuse's cloud robustness through two complementary analyses.}

\added{\textbf{Architectural robustness via quality-aware training: } Our quality-aware training framework (Section~\ref{sec:quality_aware}) explicitly incorporates Sentinel-2 Scene Classification Layer (SCL) cloud masks as per-pixel loss weights during training. Pixels flagged as cloud-contaminated (SCL classes 7--9: cloud low/medium/ high probability; class 10: thin cirrus; class 3: cloud shadow) receive down-weighted loss contributions, while SAR observations, unaffected by clouds, are weighted by backscatter reliability. This ensures the model learns to leverage SAR structure when optical signals are degraded, rather than relying on optical artefacts. Critically, this quality-aware weighting comes from independently derived metadata (SCL from Sentinel-2 processing chain; SAR quality from radiometric calibration), providing multi-source evidence of data reliability.}

\added{\textbf{Controlled cloud occlusion experiment:} To directly measure SAR's compensating role, we conduct a controlled \textit{simulated cloud occlusion} experiment: all Sentinel-2 input channels are set to zero for every test patch (simulating total optical loss), leaving Sentinel-1 SAR as the sole information source. This controlled design isolates SAR's contribution precisely and avoids confounds introduced by partial cloud coverage.}

\added{Table~\ref{tab:cloud_robustness} presents results on all 18,117 test patches. XFuse under complete S2 occlusion achieves \textbf{83.2\%} accuracy, only 8.7 percentage points below full S1+S2 performance and \textbf{higher than Early Fusion operating with its complete multi-modal input (81.3\%)}. This result demonstrates that the bidirectional cross-attention mechanism internalises complementary SAR-optical representations: when optical data is unavailable, attention weights shift to prioritise S1 features, maintaining meaningful snow discrimination. Concatenation-based baselines (Early Fusion, CNN-Only) have no analogous adaptive mechanism: they treat both modalities symmetrically and cannot compensate for a missing modality.}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering

\caption{\added{Controlled cloud occlusion experiment (all 18,117 test patches). Sentinel-2 channels zeroed to simulate complete cloud cover; model relies purely on Sentinel-1 SAR. XFuse without optical data surpasses Early Fusion operating with full multi-modal input.}}
\label{tab:cloud_robustness}
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabular}{lcc|cc}
\toprule
\textbf{Method} & \textbf{Input} & \textbf{Acc.~(\%)} & \textbf{MAE~(\%)} & \textbf{$\Delta$ Acc.} \\
\midrule
