Figure~\ref{fig:qualitative} presents representative test samples demonstrating our model's superior spatial detail preservation and uncertainty quantification. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:qualitative_comparison}, our hybrid architecture produces smoother, more realistic snow cover maps with accurate boundary delineation. Early Fusion exhibits noisy predictions with false positives in non-snow regions due to its inability to capture complex inter-modal relationships. CNN-Only achieves better smoothness but misses fine-scale snow patterns in complex terrain due to lack of global context. Transformer-Only produces spatially inconsistent results, lacking the local feature extraction necessary for precise boundary localization. XFuse preserves fine-scale snow boundaries, avoids false positives in forested areas, and captures gradual snow-cover gradients more faithfully than all baselines. The corresponding per-model error maps (Figure~\ref{fig:error_maps}) reveal that XFuse achieves substantially lower absolute errors at snow-land transition zones and forest edges --- precisely the regions where spatial context from the Transformer and bidirectional cross-attention provide the greatest benefit. The spatially coherent uncertainty maps provide operationally valuable confidence information, with elevated uncertainty at ambiguous boundaries and low uncertainty in homogeneous regions.

