\section{Conclusion}
\label{sec:conclusion}

This work addressed the critical challenge of accurate, all-weather fractional snow cover estimation through a novel hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture for multi-modal fusion of Sentinel-1 SAR (2 polarizations: VV/VH at 10m) and Sentinel-2 optical imagery (13 spectral bands unified at 10m). We introduced three principal methodological contributions that establish new paradigms for earth observation: (1) a multi-resolution processing framework implementing a novel $10\text{m} \rightarrow 60\text{m} \rightarrow 10\text{m}$ training strategy that enables learning from coarse-resolution labels (GFSC at 60m) while producing enhanced high-resolution predictions through learned aggregation and guided disaggregation, (2) a cross-modal attention fusion mechanism employing bidirectional query-key-value transformations to explicitly model inter-sensor dependencies between SAR backscatter and optical spectral responses, and (3) a quality-aware training paradigm that leverages auxiliary metadata from multiple sources (Sentinel-2 SCL, AOT, WVP; GFSC QC, QCFLAGS) through multiplicative loss weighting and curriculum learning. The integrated architecture combines dual-path CNN backbones with modality-specific attention (CBAM for SAR, ECA for optical), cross-attention fusion for intelligent multi-modal integration, and spatial transformers (6 layers, 8 heads, 512-dim) for global context modeling.

Comprehensive experimental evaluation on 29 spatially distinct Sentinel-1/2 tiles from northern Finland with rigorous 70\% train / 15\% val / 15\% test split and 30-day temporal separation demonstrated state-of-the-art performance on 18,117 test patches from 4 geographically unseen tiles. Our model achieves 13.77\% mean absolute error and 94.66\% classification accuracy with 78.71\% F1-score, representing substantial improvements over strong baselines: 38.7\% MAE reduction compared to CNN-Only (22.46\% $\rightarrow$ 13.77\%), 5.5 percentage point accuracy improvement (89.13\% $\rightarrow$ 94.66\%), and 23.0 percentage point F1-score improvement (55.67\% $\rightarrow$ 78.71\%). Statistical validation confirmed strong correlation ($r=0.784$, $p<0.001$) with coefficient of determination $R^2=0.588$ (explaining 58.8\% variance), substantially exceeding baselines: CNN-Only ($R^2=0.239$), Early Fusion ($R^2=0.026$), Transformer-Only ($R^2=0.111$). Spatial autocorrelation analysis (Moran's I=0.353, $p<0.001$) validated physically realistic spatial patterns. Systematic ablation studies confirmed each component's contribution: removing cross-attention caused 5.16 point accuracy drop (largest individual contribution), removing transformer reduced accuracy by 3.86 points, demonstrating synergistic benefits of the hybrid design. Critically, uncertainty quantification achieved excellent calibration with Expected Calibration Error (ECE) of 0.025 (well below the 0.03 threshold), mean uncertainty of 10.8\% appropriately matching observed error rate, and spatially coherent patterns (Moran's I=0.548, $p=0.002$) with elevated values at snow-land boundaries. Test-time augmentation enhanced accuracy by 2.3\% while reducing uncertainty variance by 22.4\%. Computational efficiency analysis confirmed operational feasibility: 47.1M parameters, 4.2GB GPU memory during inference, $\sim$50ms per patch on A100 GPU, enabling throughput of $\sim$2,000 patches/minute with batch processing.

The methodological advances introduced in this work establish new paradigms for earth observation applications. The multi-resolution training strategy addresses the fundamental challenge of leveraging high-resolution satellite sensors when training labels exist only at coarser resolutions, with broad applicability to land cover classification, vegetation monitoring, and urban change detection. The quality-aware learning framework provides systematic methods for learning from heterogeneous satellite archives while quantifying uncertainty, enabling responsible deployment of machine learning in operational contexts where reliability is paramount. The cross-modal attention fusion mechanism demonstrates that explicit modeling of inter-sensor dependencies significantly outperforms simpler concatenation or ensemble approaches, providing a template for future multi-sensor earth observation systems. The demonstrated calibration of uncertainty estimates (ECE=0.025) with spatially coherent patterns enables targeted improvement strategies and supports probabilistic decision-making frameworks that propagate observation uncertainty through downstream applications.

From a practical deployment perspective, this work advances operational readiness for snow monitoring systems across multiple dimensions. The processing efficiency should be considered before processing regional satellite archives since it requires specialised computational infrastructure beyond standard GPU hardware. The all-weather capability, combining SAR's cloud-penetrating observations with optical imagery's superior surface discrimination, ensures continuous monitoring independent of atmospheric conditions. The well-calibrated uncertainty estimates (ECE=0.025) provide operational systems with trustworthy quantitative confidence bounds for risk-informed decision-making in applications ranging from flood forecasting to avalanche risk assessment. The demonstrated robustness across diverse snow coverage scenarios (0.2\%-75.5\%) and ability to handle varying data quality conditions confirm reliability under real-world operational conditions. These capabilities address critical operational requirements for water resource management, where accurate snow cover information supports reservoir operation, irrigation planning, and hydropower generation decisions affecting billions of people dependent on snow-fed water supplies.

Future research directions include:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item \textbf{Multi-Season and Multi-Region Validation.} Expanding evaluation beyond northern Finland (December 2024--July 2025) to encompass diverse geographic regions (mid-latitudes, forested environments, arid zones), seasonal conditions (snowmelt periods, fall snow onset, spring transitions), and multi-year temporal coverage to comprehensively validate generalization capabilities and establish year-round operational readiness;

    \item \textbf{Operational Product Comparison.} Conducting systematic comparison with operational snow products (MODIS MOD10A1F, VIIRS VNP10A1F, Sentinel-3 SLSTR, IMS) and traditional NDSI thresholding under identical evaluation protocols to quantify advantages relative to established baselines and contextualize performance within the operational snow monitoring landscape;

    \item \textbf{Model Compression and Efficiency.} Developing model compression techniques (quantization, pruning, knowledge distillation) to reduce the 47.1M parameter footprint and 4.2GB memory requirements, enabling deployment on resource-constrained computing environments and supporting continental-scale processing at full Sentinel-1/2 temporal resolution;

    \item \textbf{Task Transferability.} Evaluating the hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture with cross-modal attention fusion on related remote sensing applications (land cover classification, change detection, crop monitoring, disaster assessment) to validate transferability and demonstrate the methodological contributions' broader applicability beyond snow cover mapping;

    \item \textbf{Additional Modalities.} Incorporating additional data sources including SAR interferometric coherence for wet snow detection, multi-temporal NDSI time series for phenological context, L-band SAR for improved dry snow discrimination, and elevation-derived terrain parameters (slope, aspect, terrain ruggedness) to further enhance prediction accuracy in complex mountainous environments; and

    \item \textbf{Hydrological Integration.} Integrating fractional snow cover predictions with process-based snowmelt models and hydrological forecasting systems to evaluate end-to-end performance for operational water resource applications and validate downstream utility for reservoir operation, irrigation planning, and flood forecasting.
\end{enumerate}

The demonstrated success of our hybrid CNN-Transformer architecture with cross-modal attention fusion, multi-resolution processing, and quality-aware learning establishes a foundation for next-generation earth observation systems. The substantial performance improvements over strong baselines (38.7\% MAE reduction, 5.5pp accuracy gain) combined with excellent uncertainty calibration (ECE=0.025) validate that explicit modeling of inter-sensor dependencies through bidirectional attention mechanisms, synergistic combination of CNNs for local feature extraction with Transformers for global context, and systematic integration of quality metadata enable robust operational deployment. These methodological advances provide a template for multi-sensor fusion systems that combine heterogeneous satellite data, handle variable quality conditions, and provide quantified uncertainties essential for responsible decision-making in critical earth observation applications during an era of accelerating environmental change.
